  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...A brief period of dangerous heat Tuesday, with \nstill very warm, but less humid conditions today and Wednesday.\n\nLarge dome of high pressure will expand east across much of the \ncentral CONUS today, where its center will strengthen to an \nimpressive 600dam over the upper Mississippi Valley and upper Great \nLakes region. The eastern periphery of this large ridge will then \nexpand east into the central and lower Great Lakes tonight and \nTuesday. \n\nIn response, surface temperatures will rise above average today, \nhowever humidity levels will remain in check. This should keep heat \nindices mainly in the mid 80s to around 90 this afternoon. Tuesday \nwill be the hottest day with 850mb temps rising into the the 22C-25C \nrange (99th percentile for mid July), with the higher-end values \nacross western NY. This will support highs in the low to mid 90s. \nWhen combined with rising humidity levels, heat index values are \nexpected to be in the mid and upper 90s across much of western and \nnorth central NY, with low 100s possible across portions of the lake \nplains, Genesee Valley, and Finger Lakes region. For these reasons \nand in collaboration with surrounding offices, a Heat Advisory has \nbeen issued for all of western and north-central NY from 11 AM to \n8 PM Tuesday.\n\nA fairly stout 30-40 knot LLJ will be located just 1-2 kft off the \ndeck Tuesday afternoon, allowing a good portion of those stronger \nwinds aloft to mix to the surface. When combined with a tightening \npressure gradient, expect to see frequent WSW to SW'erly gusts in \nthe 25-35 mph range, with a few gusts to 40 mph not out of the \nquestion northeast of Lake Erie and east through northeast of Lake \nOntario. This will at least provide some natural relief to the heat, \nespecially across those areas downwind of the lakes. That said, with \nlake surface temperatures generally in the low to mid 70s, it's \neffects will be much less pronounced than earlier in the season.\n\nVery warm and humid conditions will remain into at least the first \npart of Wednesday, before an approaching mid level trough and \nsurface cold front drop south out of eastern Canada ushering in \ncooler and drier air from north to south through the second half of \nthe day. High temps will still range through the 80s by early \nWednesday afternoon, but dropping humidity levels will keep heat \nindices about the same as the actual air temperature.",
